CAT = "cpaiscAvail"; SEG = "AVL"
Q = [
("A system has 720 hours of agreed service time in a month and is down for 9 of them. What is its availability?",
 "98.75%", ["98.25%", "99.10%", "1.25%"],
 "Availability is (agreed service time - downtime) / agreed service time: (720 - 9) / 720 = 711 / 720 = 98.75%. The 1.25% is the downtime share.",
 (720 - 9) / 720 * 100),
("A service has 500 hours of agreed service time in a quarter with 2 hours of unplanned downtime. What is its availability?",
 "99.6%", ["99.2%", "98.0%", "0.4%"],
 "Availability is (500 - 2) / 500 = 498 / 500 = 99.6%. The 0.4% is the downtime, not the availability.",
 (500 - 2) / 500 * 100),
("A full backup runs Sunday and differential backups run Monday through Thursday. The server fails Friday morning. Which backups restore it?",
 "Sunday full and Thursday differential", ["Sunday full and all four differentials", "Thursday's differential on its own", "Sunday's full backup on its own"],
 "A differential holds every change since the last full backup, so only the full and the latest differential are needed. Restoring every set is the incremental method."),
("A full backup runs Sunday and incremental backups run Monday through Thursday. The server fails Friday morning. How many backup sets must be restored?",
 "5 sets", ["2 sets", "4 sets", "1 set"],
 "Each incremental holds only the changes since the previous backup, so the restore needs the Sunday full plus all 4 incrementals: 1 + 4 = 5 sets.",
 1 + 4),
("Which backup type usually takes the least time to run each night?",
 "Incremental", ["Differential", "Full", "Full with verify"],
 "An incremental copies only what changed since the last backup of any kind, so it is the smallest each night. A differential keeps growing until the next full backup."),
("The point in time to which data must be recoverable after an outage, which sets the acceptable data loss, is the:",
 "Recovery point objective", ["Recovery time objective", "Maximum tolerable downtime", "Mean time to repair"],
 "NIST SP 800-34 defines the RPO as the point in time before a disruption to which data can be recovered. The RTO is how long the system may stay down."),
("Which measure is the total time a system owner will accept a business process to be disrupted?",
 "Maximum tolerable downtime", ["Recovery time objective", "Recovery point objective", "Mean time between failures"],
 "The MTD is the total outage the owner will accept, including all impacts. The RTO is the time to restore the system and must fit within the MTD."),
("Under NIST SP 800-34, the first step of a business impact analysis is to:",
 "Find processes and their criticality", ["Test the recovery plan procedures", "Choose alternate site strategies", "Identify the recovery priorities"],
 "The BIA's three steps are determine mission and business processes and recovery criticality, identify resource requirements, then identify recovery priorities. Testing comes after a plan exists."),
("Writing every change to two disks at the same moment so either can carry on if the other fails is:",
 "Mirroring", ["Striping", "Tape rotation", "Incremental backup"],
 "Mirroring keeps an identical copy in real time. Striping spreads data across disks for speed and gives no redundancy on its own."),
("Which replication approach confirms each write only after the remote copy has also been updated?",
 "Synchronous replication", ["Asynchronous replication", "Scheduled snapshot copy", "Nightly batch transfer"],
 "Synchronous replication waits for both sites, so no committed data is lost if the primary fails. Asynchronous replication sends changes after the fact and may lose the latest writes."),
("A recovery site with equipment and current data that can take over within hours is a:",
 "Hot site", ["Cold site", "Warm site", "Reciprocal agreement"],
 "A hot site is fully equipped and current. A warm site has some equipment but needs data and setup, and a cold site is space and utilities only."),
("Which plan focuses on restoring IT systems after a disruption, rather than keeping the whole business operating?",
 "Disaster recovery plan", ["Business continuity plan", "Incident response plan", "Crisis communications plan"],
 "The disaster recovery plan restores IT systems and data. The business continuity plan is broader, covering how the whole organization keeps running."),
("Under the Trust Services Criteria, monitoring current processing capacity so the system can meet demand addresses which criterion?",
 "A1.1", ["PI1.3", "CC7.2", "C1.1"],
 "A1.1 says the entity maintains, monitors and evaluates current processing capacity and use of system components to manage capacity demand. CC7.2 is monitoring for security anomalies."),
("In a SOC 2 examination covering availability, the service auditor finds the recovery plan has never been tested. Which criterion is most directly not met?",
 "A1.3, recovery plan testing", ["A1.1, capacity monitoring", "A1.2, environmental protections", "CC8.1, change management"],
 "A1.3 says the entity tests recovery plan procedures supporting system recovery. A1.2 covers environmental protections, backups and recovery infrastructure."),
("Backups run every night but no one has ever restored one. Which risk does this leave unaddressed?",
 "Backups may not be usable", ["Backups may run too often", "Storage costs may rise", "The RTO may be too long"],
 "Only a test restore proves that backups are complete and readable. Without one, the entity discovers a bad backup during the disaster."),
("Why should at least one backup copy be kept at a separate location?",
 "One site event can't hit every copy", ["It allows faster local restores", "It removes the need to test restores", "It keeps the nightly backup window short"],
 "An off-site copy survives a fire, flood or theft at the main site. Off-site copies usually make restores slower, and they still need test restores."),
]
